---
// G2 and G4, network classification (Ch. 9). G2: one rule on the talk's three networks (src/scripts/defence/trio.ts);
// under each network three histograms, empty until ▶, then computed live from the last rounds (./print): the Shannon
// entropy and the Lempel–Ziv complexity of every node's row, as Miranda et al. 2016 define them, and the neighbourhood
// density that Ch. 9 used; then a fingerprint in their place (a whorl, a loop, a tented arch), and the message: we look
// for rules whose fingerprints differ. First the consensus-seeking rule: every network agrees, and the three fingerprints
// are one (a plain arch); then Miranda et al.'s rule from the same start, and three different prints. G4: the same two rules, every
// change of answer marked: jaggedness, our result, and the bridge to what consensus-seeking rules are good for instead.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import { phi } from './tex';
import { TRIO } from '../../scripts/defence/trio';
const rows = [
  { f: 'entropy', name: 'Shannon entropy' },
  { f: 'lz', name: 'Lempel–Ziv complexity' },
  { f: 'density', name: 'neighbourhood density' },
];
const rules = [
  { id: 'consensus', name: 'Consensus-seeking', r: 9, B: 488, S: 464 },
  { id: 'miranda', name: 'Miranda et al.', r: 9, B: 170, S: 340 },
];
const sides = [
  { x: { r: 9, B: 488, S: 464 }, name: 'A consensus-seeking rule' },
  { x: { r: 9, B: 170, S: 340 }, name: 'Miranda et al.’s rule' },
];

// G2's fingerprints, one textbook pattern per network (src/scripts/defence/fingerprint.ts); all three become a plain
// arch once the networks look alike
const kinds = ['whorl', 'loop', 'tented'];
---
<Slide id="ch-classification" label="Which network is this?" builds={5} class="g2" ch={9}>
  <div class="g2-head">
    <h2>Which network is this?</h2>
    <div class="g2-rules" role="group" aria-label="Rule">
      {rules.map((r, i) => <button type="button" class="ctl" data-rule={r.id} aria-pressed={i ? 'false' : 'true'}>{r.name} <span set:html={phi(r.r, r.B, r.S)} /></button>)}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="stack g2-lede">
    <p data-b-off="5">One rule, three networks. Does what it does give the network away?</p>
    <p data-b="5">We look for rules whose fingerprints are clearly <b>different</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="g2-grid">
    {rows.map((r, k) => <p class="g2-row" data-b="1" style={`grid-row: ${k + 3}`}>{r.name}</p>)}
    {TRIO.map((ty, c) => (
      <figure data-type={c} style={`grid-column: ${c + 2}`}>
        <figcaption>{ty.name}</figcaption>
        <div class="g2-net">
          <canvas class="net" aria-label={`the rule running on the ${ty.name} network`}></canvas>
          {c === 2 && <SimButton class="g2-btn" />}
        </div>
        {rows.map((r, k) => <canvas class="hist" style={`grid-row: ${k + 3}`} data-f={r.f} data-b="1" aria-label={`${r.name} of the ${ty.name} network, over the last rounds`}></canvas>)}
        <div class="fp" data-b-only="3 5" aria-hidden="true">
          <canvas class="fp-own" data-kind={kinds[c]} data-seed={11 + c}></canvas>
          <canvas class="fp-same" data-kind="arch" data-seed="14"></canvas>
        </div>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The first application turns the question around: instead of asking what a rule does on a network, ask what it tells us about the network. Here one rule runs on the three networks of before, a random one, a small-world ring and a scale-free one, 150 nodes each: first the consensus-seeking rule of the previous slides. One click names what we will look at, three properties per network, still empty; the next starts the rule.</p>
  <p slot="say">To turn what the network does into numbers, look at every node over the last 40 rounds. How evenly does it split its time between on and off: its Shannon entropy. How many new pieces does it take to write its row of ons and offs down: its Lempel–Ziv complexity. And what share of its neighbours is on, round after round: the neighbourhood density, which Chapter 9 used. Each network gets three histograms, live on the slide, filling up within a few seconds. Under the consensus-seeking rule every network soon agrees on all off, every node does the same, and the histograms of the three networks become the same.</p>
  <p slot="say">Together, the three histograms are the network’s fingerprint (drawn here as a real one), and a simple classifier can learn to tell the fingerprints apart. Under the consensus-seeking rule the three fingerprints are one and the same: a bad detective. The next click puts in the rule G. H. B. Miranda and colleagues used in 2016, when they started classifying networks this way, <Fragment set:html={phi(9, 170, 340)} /> on their own intervals (closed on the left, so not quite our symmetric ones), and runs it from the same start. Now the histograms differ: on the scale-free network its many nodes with only four links soon stay off for good, so its entropy has a second peak at 0. And so do the fingerprints: a whorl, a loop and a tented arch, the textbook kinds. So we look for rules whose fingerprints are clearly different; the switch at the top does the same by hand. The thesis did this on 23 datasets, with histograms split by degree and a support-vector machine; this slide keeps the idea and shrinks the rest.</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Jaggedness" builds={2} class="g4" ch={9}>
  <h2>Jaggedness: how often a rule changes its mind</h2>
  <div class="g4-two">
    {sides.map((sd, i) => (
      <figure class="jag" data-rule={i}>
        <p class="g4-name">{sd.name} <span set:html={phi(sd.x.r, sd.x.B, sd.x.S)} /></p>
        <canvas class="jdiag" data-rule={`${sd.x.r},${sd.x.B},${sd.x.S}`} aria-label="the rule with every change between neighbouring intervals marked"></canvas>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="result" data-b="1"><b>Our result</b> On 13 of 23 datasets, more jagged rules tend to classify better. Searching by jaggedness finds a rule within 5% of the best while trying only 10–20% of the rules.</p>
  <p class="bridge" data-b="2">Consensus-seeking rules make bad detectives. But they are good at something else: <b>finding who matters</b>.</p>
  <p slot="say">What makes the difference can be read off the rule itself. Walk along the intervals, from few neighbours on to all of them, and count how often the rule changes its answer: that is its jaggedness. The consensus-seeking rule changes its mind at 38% of the boundaries, Miranda’s rule at 94%. A jagged rule is a sensitive rule: a small change in the neighbourhood often changes what a node does.</p>
  <p slot="say">This is our contribution to network classification, Chapter 9, work led by Lucas C. de Oliveira (with M. Rollier, J. M. Baetens and O. M. Bruno, under review). On 13 of the 23 datasets, more jagged rules tend to classify better (correlations between about 0.2 and 0.46), and the best rules sit in a middle-to-high band of jaggedness. That makes jaggedness a guide: searching by jaggedness finds a rule within 5% of the best while trying only 10 to 20% of the rules. On social and bioinformatics networks there is no such pattern, but the method itself is mediocre there as well.</p>
  <p slot="say">So consensus-seeking rules make bad detectives. But they turn out to be good at something else: finding which nodes matter.</p>
</Slide>

<style>
  .g2-head { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-rules { display: flex; gap: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-rules .ctl { font-size: calc(28 * var(--u)); padding: calc(8 * var(--u)) calc(18 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-rules .ctl[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--sun); }
  .g2-lede { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: calc(250 * var(--u)) repeat(3, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: calc(44 * var(--u)) calc(330 * var(--u)) repeat(3, calc(86 * var(--u))); gap: calc(10 * var(--u)) calc(40 * var(--u));
  }
  .g2-grid figure { margin: 0; grid-row: 1 / span 5; display: grid; grid-template-rows: subgrid; justify-items: center; position: relative; }
  .g2-grid figure > * { grid-column: 1; }
  .g2-grid figcaption { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; grid-row: 1; }
  .g2-net { position: relative; grid-row: 2; }
  .net { width: calc(330 * var(--u)); height: calc(330 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-net :global(.g2-btn) { right: calc(-70 * var(--u)); bottom: 0; }
  .hist { width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  .g2-row { grid-column: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; font-size: calc(30 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.15; font-weight: 700; }
  /* the fingerprint takes the place of the histograms, which fade far back */
  .fp { grid-row: 3 / span 3; width: 100%; height: 100%; position: relative; pointer-events: none; }
  .fp canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; transition: opacity 0.6s ease; }
  :global(.g2.alike) .fp-own, :global(.g2:not(.alike)) .fp-same { opacity: 0; }
  :global(.g2.printed) .hist { opacity: 0.12; }
  :global(.g2.printed) .g2-row { opacity: 0.35; }

  .g4-two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: calc(80 * var(--u)); }
  .jag { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .g4-name { font-size: calc(38 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; }
  .jdiag { width: calc(820 * var(--u)); height: calc(250 * var(--u)); }
  :global(.g4) .result { margin-top: auto; }
  .bridge { font-size: calc(44 * var(--u)); font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.25; }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, buildOf, type How } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker, type Stage } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { label } from '../../scripts/defence/plot';
  import { picture, drawNet, type Picture } from '../../scripts/defence/netdraw';
  import { TRIO, trioNet, trioStart } from '../../scripts/defence/trio';
  import { fingerprinter, type Feature, type Print } from '../../scripts/defence/print';
  import { type Rule, step } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { ridges, drawPrint, type PrintKind } from '../../scripts/defence/fingerprint';

  const RULES: Record<string, Rule> = { miranda: { r: 9, B: 170, S: 340, part: 'uni' }, consensus: { r: 9, B: 488, S: 464 } };
  const FEATS: Feature[] = ['entropy', 'lz', 'density'];

  /** One histogram as bars on a common height `top`; nothing yet while its window is empty. */
  function bars(st: Stage, h: number[] | null, top: number) {
    const { ctx, t } = st, base = st.h - 4, bw = st.w / (h?.length ?? 10);
    ctx.strokeStyle = t.muted; ctx.lineWidth = 2; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(0, base); ctx.lineTo(st.w, base); ctx.stroke();
    if (!h) return;
    ctx.fillStyle = t.ink;
    h.forEach((v, b) => { const y = base - (Math.min(v, top) / top) * (st.h - 10); ctx.fillRect(b * bw + 2, y, bw - 4, base - y); });
  }

  // ── G2: the three networks, run by one rule; their fingerprints, live ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.g2')) {
    const figs = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('figure[data-type]')), buttons = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rule]'));
    let pick = 'consensus', pics: Picture[] | null = null;
    // the networks are built when the slide is first drawn, not at page load
    const pic = () => (pics ??= TRIO.map((_, k) => picture(trioNet(k), 330, 10, k === 1 ? 3.2 : 3.8, k === 2 ? 0.6 : false)));
    type Col = { net: HTMLCanvasElement; hists: HTMLCanvasElement[]; a: Uint8Array; b: Uint8Array; fp: ReturnType<typeof fingerprinter> | null; print: Print };
    const cols: Col[] = figs.map((f) => ({ net: f.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!, hists: FEATS.map((x) => f.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>(`canvas.hist[data-f="${x}"]`)!), a: new Uint8Array(0), b: new Uint8Array(0), fp: null, print: { entropy: null, lz: null, density: null } }));
    const reset = () => cols.forEach((c, k) => {
      const p = pic()[k];
      c.a = trioStart(k, p.net.n); c.b = new Uint8Array(p.net.n);
      c.fp ??= fingerprinter(p.net); c.fp.reset(); c.fp.push(c.a); c.print = c.fp.print();
    });
    function paint() {
      const ps = pic();
      cols.forEach((c, k) => { const st = stage(c.net); if (st) drawNet(st, ps[k], c.a); });
      // each row on one height, so the three columns compare
      FEATS.forEach((f, r) => {
        const top = Math.max(0.2, ...cols.map((c) => Math.max(0, ...(c.print[f] ?? [0]))));
        cols.forEach((c) => { const st = stage(c.hists[r]); if (st) bars(st, c.print[f], top); });
      });
    }
    const rule = () => RULES[pick];
    // twelve rounds a second, so the histograms take their shape within a few seconds
    const round = () => cols.forEach((c, k) => { step(c.a, pic()[k].net, rule(), c.b); [c.a, c.b] = [c.b, c.a]; c.fp!.push(c.a); });
    const run = ticker(s, 12, () => { round(); cols.forEach((c) => (c.print = c.fp!.print())); }, paint);
    // sixty rounds at once, so a step back or a jump lands on histograms that have taken their shape
    const settle = () => { for (let t = 0; t < 60; t++) round(); cols.forEach((c) => (c.print = c.fp!.print())); };
    // the prints follow the rule: one and the same under the consensus-seeking rule, one of each kind under Miranda's
    const choose = (id: string) => {
      buttons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.rule === id)));
      s.classList.toggle('alike', id === 'consensus');
      if (id === pick) return;
      pick = id; reset(); paint();
    };
    buttons.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => choose(b.dataset.rule!)));
    const going = sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, 2, {
      reset() { run.play(false); reset(); if (buildOf(s) >= 3) settle(); paint(); },
      play() { run.play(true); },
    });
    slide(s, {
      // the consensus-seeking rule up to build 3, where its prints take the histograms' place; build 4 takes them away
      // and runs Miranda et al.'s rule from the start; build 5 shows its prints. A step back over build 4, or a jump,
      // lands on the other rule with its histograms formed.
      set(b: number, how: How) {
        s.classList.toggle('printed', b === 3 || b === 5);
        const want = b >= 4 ? 'miranda' : 'consensus';
        if (want === pick) return;
        choose(want);
        if (how === 'next') { run.play(true); going(true); }
        else if (b >= 2) { settle(); paint(); if (how === 'prev') run.play(true); }
      },
      enter() { pick = ''; choose('consensus'); },
      leave() { run.play(false); },
    });
    reset();
    for (const c of cols) { onSize(c.net, paint); c.hists.forEach((h) => onSize(h, paint)); }
    // the prints, as tall as the three histograms together
    for (const c of s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('.fp canvas')) {
      const lines = ridges(c.dataset.kind as PrintKind, +c.dataset.seed!);
      onSize(c, () => { const st = stage(c); if (!st) return; const h = st.h - 4; drawPrint(st.ctx, lines, (st.w - h / 1.25) / 2, 2, h, st.t.ink); });
    }
  }

  /** The rule as two rows of intervals, born (top) and survive (bottom), with a red tick at every change of answer. */
  function diagram(st: Stage, x: { r: number; B: number; S: number }, left: number) {
    const { ctx, t } = st, gap = 30, top = 10, w = (st.w - left) / x.r, h = (st.h - gap - top - 50) / 2;
    label(st, 'born', left - 14, top + h / 2, { size: 28, align: 'right', color: t.muted });
    label(st, 'survives', left - 14, top + gap + 1.5 * h, { size: 28, align: 'right', color: t.muted });
    let changes = 0;
    for (const [row, mask, col] of [[0, x.B, t.accent], [1, x.S, t.ink]] as const) {
      const Y = top + row * (h + gap);
      for (let i = 0; i < x.r; i++) {
        const on = (mask >> i) & 1, X = left + i * w;
        ctx.fillStyle = on ? col : t.panel; ctx.fillRect(X + 2, Y, w - 4, h);
        if (!on) { ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 2; ctx.strokeRect(X + 3, Y + 1, w - 6, h - 2); }
        if (i > 0 && ((mask >> i) & 1) !== ((mask >> (i - 1)) & 1)) {
          changes++;
          ctx.strokeStyle = t.danger; ctx.lineWidth = 7; ctx.lineCap = 'round';
          ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(X, Y - 8); ctx.lineTo(X, Y + h + 8); ctx.stroke();
        }
      }
    }
    label(st, `jaggedness ${Math.round((100 * changes) / (2 * (x.r - 1)))}%`, left, st.h - 18, { size: 32, color: t.danger, weight: 700 });
  }

  // ── G4: the two rules of G2, every change of answer between neighbouring intervals marked ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.g4')) {
    s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.jdiag').forEach((c) => {
      const [r, B, S] = c.dataset.rule!.split(',').map(Number);
      onSize(c, () => { const st = stage(c); if (st) diagram(st, { r, B, S }, 150); });
    });
  }
</script>
